    image: { src: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/27/CairoEgMuseumTaaMaskMostlyPhotographed.jpg", credit: "Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CairoEgMuseumTaaMaskMostlyPhotographed.jpg", alt: "CairoEgMuseumTaaMaskMostlyPhotographed" },
    desc: "The <b>Mask of Tutankhamun</b> is the funerary mask of beaten and burnished gold that was laid over the head and shoulders of the mummified king inside the innermost of his three nested coffins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Howard Carter's team had found the tomb in November 1922, but the mask itself was not uncovered until October 1925, by which time the resins poured over the body at burial had set hard enough to make the coffins very difficult to separate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It stands about 54 centimetres (21 inches) high and weighs roughly 10 kilograms (22 pounds), the face framed by the striped <i>nemes</i> headcloth and inlaid with lapis lazuli, obsidian, quartz, carnelian and coloured glass, with a vulture and a cobra rearing from the brow as the protecting goddesses of Upper and Lower Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A spell from the <i>Book of the Dead</i> runs across the shoulders and back, naming the parts of the king's body one by one and identifying each with a god, so the mask is ritual equipment as much as it is a portrait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its plaited beard was knocked off and hurriedly glued back with epoxy in 2014, a repair that had to be undone and done again properly the following year.",
