    desc: "The <b>Bust of Nefertiti</b> is a painted limestone and stucco portrait of the great royal wife of Akhenaten, made around 1345 BCE and found in the ruins of the sculptor Thutmose's workshop at Amarna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A German expedition under Ludwig Borchardt lifted it on 6 December 1912; it stands about 48 centimetres (19 inches) high, weighs some 20 kilograms (44 pounds), and wears a flat-topped blue crown that appears on no other Egyptian queen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The right eye holds an inlay of quartz with a black-painted pupil and the left has none at all, which has been read as later damage, as an unfinished state, and as evidence that the piece was a master model kept in the studio for copying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has been in Berlin since 1913 and is now the centrepiece of the Neues Museum, and Egypt has asked for it back more than once, arguing that the division of finds which allowed it to leave was improperly conducted. Claims that it is a modern forgery surface periodically and are rejected by specialists, and computed tomography has revealed a second, subtly different face carved in the limestone core beneath the plaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
