    desc: "The <b>Nebra Sky Disc</b> is a bronze disc about 30 centimetres (12 inches) across, inlaid with gold shapes usually read as the sun or a full moon, a crescent, and a scatter of 32 stars among which a cluster of seven is taken for the Pleiades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two gold arcs were added at its edges later, each spanning 82 degrees — the angle between midsummer and midwinter sunset seen from that latitude — and a curved shape often called a boat was added along the bottom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Looters dug it up in 1999 on the Mittelberg hill near Nebra in Saxony-Anhalt along with swords, axes and bracelets, and it was recovered only in 2002 in a police operation in Basel; it is now in the State Museum of Prehistory in Halle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bronzes buried with it place the hoard at about 1600 BCE, in the Early Bronze Age Únětice culture, though an argument published in 2021 that the disc is a thousand years younger was quickly and firmly contested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether it served to reconcile the lunar and solar years or simply pictured the sky is disputed, but it is among the oldest concrete images of the night sky known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
