    desc: "The <b>Sutton Hoo Helmet</b> is an iron helmet faced with tinned bronze panels, from the ship burial under Mound 1 at Sutton Hoo in Suffolk, dug in the summer of 1939 weeks before the outbreak of war and now in the British Museum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has a full face mask with garnet-set eyebrows and a gilded dragon's head over the nose, and the whole front reads as a bird in flight one way and as a dragon the other, while stamped panels around it show interlaced animals and warriors. The helmet came out of the ground in some 500 corroded fragments, was reconstructed by Herbert Maryon in the 1940s, and was taken apart and rebuilt again by Nigel Williams in 1970 and 1971 once the first attempt was shown to be wrong in several places. The burial it belongs to is extraordinarily rich — a ship 27 metres (89 feet) long dragged up from the river and filled with weapons, silver and gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No body was identified, and although the man commemorated is often supposed to be Rædwald, king of the East Angles, who died around 625, nothing in the grave names anybody.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
