    desc: "The <b>Standard of Ur</b> is a hollow wooden box inlaid with shell, red limestone and lapis lazuli set in bitumen, made around 2600 BCE and found in the Royal Cemetery at Ur in what is now southern Iraq.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is small — about 21.6 centimetres (8.5 inches) high and 49.5 centimetres (19.5 inches) long — and its two long trapezoidal sides carry the scenes conventionally called War and Peace, each set out in three registers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war side reads from the bottom up: chariots drawn by onagers trampling the fallen, infantry in cloaks and helmets, and stripped prisoners brought before a larger figure who is presumably the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The peace side shows animals, fish and produce carried in procession to a banquet where seated men drink to the music of a lyre, and it is one of the fullest pictures of Sumerian court life to survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Leonard Woolley, who excavated it in the late 1920s, guessed that it had been carried on a pole and gave it the name it still bears, though it may as easily have been the sound box of an instrument or a piece of furniture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
