    desc: "The <b>Code of Hammurabi</b> survives on a black basalt stele more than 2.2 metres (7 feet 4 inches) tall, cut with some 282 rulings in Akkadian cuneiform in the name of the king of Babylon who reigned in the 18th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the top Hammurabi stands before a seated god, generally identified as Shamash, the sun god and god of justice, who holds out the rod and ring of authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rulings are cast as cases rather than principles — if a man does this, then that follows — and they scale the penalty to the rank of the person harmed, so that an injury to a free man and the same injury to a slave are not priced alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not the oldest law collection known: the laws of Ur-Nammu are some three centuries older, and there are several others in between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The stele was found at Susa in Iran in the winter of 1901 to 1902, carried there as loot from a Babylonian city by an Elamite king around 1150 BCE, and it has been in the Louvre ever since.",
