    desc: "The <b>Cyrus Cylinder</b> is a barrel-shaped clay document about 22.5 centimetres (9 inches) long, written in Akkadian cuneiform after the Persian king Cyrus the Great took Babylon in 539 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In it Cyrus presents himself as chosen by Marduk, the city's own god, to end the misrule of the previous king Nabonidus, and he records restoring temples, returning displaced gods to their sanctuaries and letting deported peoples go home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hormuzd Rassam found it at Babylon in 1879 during excavations for the British Museum, where it remains on display, and two further fragments of the same text were later identified on a tablet in Yale's collection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has often been called the first charter of human rights, a description promoted in the 20th century and rejected by Assyriologists, who point out that it belongs to a long-established Mesopotamian genre in which a new ruler advertises his own piety and blackens his predecessor. Its historical value is real but narrower than the slogan suggests: it is the conqueror's own account of his conquest, and it says nothing directly about the Jews or about Jerusalem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
